One more comment I missed the first time around:

On Sun, 26 Dec 1999 18:24:28 +0100, Zartan <randombytes@uml.net> wrote:
>Most people want a computer to do more than number crunching, like
>networking, compiling, processing large amounts of data. My AXP gives
>terrible slowdowns when I move the mouse, so I have to spend 150 bucks for
>memory to get my mouse working properly in X? I don't think so. That money
>can well be saved for a bigger monitor or another 8 gig HD.

You need 64 meg more memory to get your mouse working? Anyone trying to run
Linux and X in 16 meg of RAM is insane, no matter what platform. If you're
paying $150 for two 16 meg SIMMs, you're overpaying badly; check out
www.priceline.com for much better deals. As for an 8 gig HD, only IDE drives
are available for that, and those impose worse slowdown on your system than
anything else. I refuse to build a system on an IDE base. IDE forces your
CPU to sit around waiting for I/Os to complete instead of going off and
doing other work. Here, as elsewhere, you get what you pay for. SCSI is more
than worth the extra cost in what it pays back in performance.



