The ole Alpha vs x86 debate is a funny thing. Like you, Zartan, my
first Alpha-based computer was a PCI64 (aka "noname", very similar
to the motherboard of the Multias) that I bought from onsale.com for
about $100 back in '97. I spent another $150 for 16meg of parity RAM
and tossed it into an old case with a full-height 1gig SCSI disk--this
was when I was a sophomore in college, and I could not afford a 
halfway decent Alpha but I really wanted to try Linux on Alpha and
generally putz around with anything different than x86's. Man, is that
old noname board SLOW (it is a 21066@166mhz w/o L2 cache). *HOWEVER*,
it is STILL DOING what I set it up to do two and a half years ago:
serving as my house's dial-up IP-MASQ server to about five PC's, Macs,
and assorted old Sparcs and MIPS-based orphaned hardware I like to play
with, not to mention acting as a PRIMARY DOMAIN CONTROLLER for all
of my (family's) Windows machines and NFS/SAMBA fileserver--all on
a dog-slow, 1994-era 21066 with 16 megs of RAM. Try *that* on a
1994-vintage 486 with 16 megs running Windows NT (it actually might
work OK if the 486 were running Linux, as well).

This past August I found another surplussed Alpha motherboard/CPU:
the PC164's that were being sold on eBay for $150. I snapped one up,
bought a shiny new ATX case, spent $250 for 128 megs of parity RAM
and threw another old SCSI disk on it with a 53c800-based host adapter,
and voila--I was *AMAZED* how fast the original PC164's are. This is
a machine that was manufactured in 1996, but runs emulation software
(M.A.M.E., if you must know) as quickly or MORE quickly than the
Pentium II machine sitting next to it (vintage 1998). Of course, if
you want performance you should have bought a 21164A or 21264-based
machine (the former can be had, like mine, for under $200), and not
expect to get performance out of a machine designed to be low-performance
when it was NEW back in 1994 (the 21066-based machine were all designed
to be the LOW-END of the Alpha spectrum).

I hope you find a good niche for that Multia, as I have. And I hope
you can realize that the later generations of Alpha are really nice,
fast, machines.

Ryan

