Jay Maynard wrote:

> Anyone trying to run Linux and X in 16 meg of RAM is insane, no matter what
> platform.

I remember having "only" 24 MB of memory on my Pentium 100 box and in X it
hardly ever swapped. Because of curiosity, I booted the system with the mem=16m
option and it still worked very fast. Everything ran perfectly and now I have
more RAM, it works like a dream. I heard the Alpha needs 128 MB memory to fully
squeeze the power out of the system. On an Intel, 16 MB is the limit for Linux
with X and if you go 32 MB or more it's super-smooth.

> If you're paying $150 for two 16 meg SIMMs, you're overpaying badly; check
> out www.priceline.com for much better deals.

No, I meant two SIMMs of 32 MB each.

> As for an 8 gig HD, only IDE drives are available for that, and those impose
> worse slowdown on your system than anything else. I refuse to build a system
> on an IDE base. IDE forces your CPU to sit around waiting for I/Os to
> complete instead of going off and doing other work. Here, as elsewhere, you
> get what you pay for. SCSI is more than worth the extra cost in what it pays
> back in performance.

The IDE-controller of the AXPpci33 and Multia is extremely slow. On my five
year old Intel motherboard
I can transfer 4 MB/sec on my 1 GB IDE hard disk and the same for my newer hard
disk.

I'm not against Alpha or something, the newer ones are great. A friend of mine
has 600 Mhz Alpha, but it needs to be clear to new buyers that Multias and
AXPpci33's need absurdly much memory to run smoothly and the IDE-controller on
those motherboards are no good, while it's performance is not amazing. As I
said before, 16 MB *is* reasonable for an Intel Linux box, 64 MB is really much
for an x86 Linux box and certainly not the limit. IDE hard disks are very fast
on an x86 box. If you can lend a Pentium from a friend, try Linux on it, you'll
see that the performance is very satisfying. I bought the AXPpci33 just because
of curiosity and ran Linux on it for some time and as the HOWTO states, the
AXPpci33 is a low-performance Alpha. If I'd ever buy a non x86, I'd rather buy
a Sun box or an SGI. I've used Sun boxes for four years now and they are really
fast and versatile.




