The "family" may be represented by "DIGITAL Personal Workstation au
series 433a, 500au, 600au."  It's using the Pyxis chipset; it appears
that this is a "miata," as far as MILO selection is concerned.

This of course is not the problem.

The problem is that the box is a refurb, and either:

a) Is broken, or
b) Has something funny in the startup procedure that is not
   self-evident. 

The former appears more likely.

I hook up monitor, keyboard, and mouse.  (Recalling that it was
reportedly a Bad Thing to power up a Multia sans keyboard.)  And then
power up the box.

- The keyboard lights flash once, and go out.  Never more to do
  anything more.  Caps Lock and Num Lock have no apparent effect on
  anything.

- The monitor never displays *anything.*

  On the Multia, I was accustomed to the TGA waiting a bit to display
  anything, and popping up some "system check" stuff before ARC got
  started.

- No useful comments on the Ethernet port...  I've not connected
  anything to it.

<warning against Egghead>
The box unfortunately came in an hour before I headed off on a week's
Christmas vacation, which means that I unfortunately have no recourse
to Egghead for having received a "dud;" the recourse is to a 90 day
Compaq warrantee whereby I call Compaq's "Replacement Parts" division.
</warning against Egghead>

My main theory on what's wrong is that the video card may be bad; I've
not opened the unit up yet.  Any other suggestions?

The *?!@#*?* Egghead people didn't include *any* kind of documentation
with it, and Compaq's web site is rather useless at coping with
anything sold pre-takeover.  One of the more useful things might be to
request replacement documentation, which will no doubt be vastly
easier if I have a part number for 433 UA docs...

