After several months of trying to sucessfully set up a DEC Multia running
RH6 as a firewall, I still have yet to find work-arounds for two persistent,
annoying problems that have plagued me since day one.

First is the well-documented 'scsi_abort' problem that tends to occur with
the ncr53c810 controller.  After swapping out every piece of hardware
(except the controller itself), rebuilding the SCSI enclosure and checking
all terminations, cables, etc., and setting the transfer mode to
asynchronous, the problem remains.  I can't write to the disk, which renders
the machine all but useless.

Second is another documented issue involving a network outage.  The network
on the internal interface (eth1) tends to go down every 15 minutes or so
(ping returns 'destination host unreachable').  It comes back up eventually,
but this is aggravating, particularly since I am usually telnet-ed into that
machine.  I have read that this could be related to problems with routed
(which I'm not running) or the driver for the card (tulip in this case).  I
have switched drivers, disabled routed, and even upgraded to kernel version
2.2.13 (which supposedly fixed a routing bug) to to avail.

I have come to expect much more reliabilty from linux, so this situation is
quite discouraging.  People talk at length about how linux will perform
certain tasks with great efficieny and reliabilty even on old, outdated
hardware.  My experience so far has proved that this is not always the case.

If anybody has had better luck with a different version or distribution of
linux running on a Multia, please let me know.  Most of my other experiences
with linux have been much better than this--although this is my first time
running it on an alpha.  I'd love to get this Multia up and running, because
it would be a great firewall machine.

The specs on the machine are:

DEC UDB Multia 166Mhz
ncr53c810 SCSI controller
Netgear FA310TX NIC (tulip driver version 0.91, I believe)
RH 6.0 (kernel 2.2.13)


Thanks in advance,
Nate Slater


