: be selected by the sym53c8xx driver or not, perhaps it's
: worth a try.

As clearly stated in the mentioned documentation 810 is NOT supported
by sym53c8xx, unless this happens to be 810A.


: Have you tried the Generic DEC ethernet driver?  I think it's the
: de4x5.o module and is supposted to be somewhat more reliable
: than the tulip driver (which is alleged to be generally braindead
: in this forum).

The only braindead things are general but incorrect statements.

'de4x5' works fine with older Tulip based cards using the first
generation chip.  When it works then, indeed, works quite well.  However
there are various newer cards for which it is not only "more reliable"
but it will not work at all.  There exist (allegedly, I still have to
see one and I dealt with quite a few of those, but serious people
reported occurences) also some tulip cards which are happy only with
0.89h driver - hence 'OLD_TULIP' option for the latest 2.2.14pre
kernels.  Fairly recently in a tulip driver there was discovered a bug
which could be responsible for cards lockups under heavy traffic.
Versions used in current 2.2.14pre have this bug fixed.

On the top of it there are on the market various cheap clones with
quality and "minor details" which may fluctuate quite wildly from
a series to series within, nominally, the same model.

All in all this is a bit of a mess. OTOH I am using tulip cards
quite extensively myself.  This is from a machine which serves
as a gateway to my whole home network to the "wide world"
(and serves currently two different IP interfaces via "alias"):

