: I don't believe that linux/alpha needs any patches to support large amounts
: of memory.  The nGB limits are because of 32 bit archs...

The current 2.2 kernels will support 2 GB memory on board without any
patches - I think that on all Alpha boards.  This limit is indeed a
result of a 32-bit architecture; that of a PCI bus and a way like
current Linux kernels are "windowing" that PCI memory in its own address
space.

Until relatively recently this was not an issue as alpha boards on which
you can run Linux, and which were ever officially certified to take more
that 1.5 GB of memory, started to appear only these days.  In order to
break that barrier various PCI drivers have to be rewritten to use
scatter/gather techniques and the work on this is actually going right
now.  Assorted PCI devices with hidden hardware assumptions that they
will be used only on Wintel boxes, and cutting corners in PCI specs, may
not survive that transition.

The way you can currently support more memory with Linux, and other
OSes, on x86 machines is "segments once again".  This is not likely be a
long term solution for serious uses.

  Michal




