Now repeat the same experiment under Tru64 and watch results. :-)

There is no magic in overlayed partitions.  All encompassing partition
'c' is really needed as a way to make the whole disk accesible to OS.
Because Linux can read (and write, but do not try that at home without a
profesional supervision :-) to things like /dev/sda there is no real
need for it other than compatibility reasons.  If you plan to use that
disk under Linux only then indeed such partition is superflous but you
still can have one.  BTW, if you really want to run mkfs on /dev/sda
then you can but better play such games on an empty disk.

