Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha,comp.windows.x

OK, here is my (quick and dirty) solution to the problem.

Reminder:

  The problem occurs with an Elsa Gloria Synergy (Permedia 2) video card
  on an XP1000.  The Xserver (XFree86-3DLabs-3.3.5-17.alpha) reports a
  maximum allowed dot-clock of 110MHz:

  (--) GLINT: Maximum allowed dot-clock: 110.000 MHz

  This is incorrect and means that one is stuck with low-resolution
  modes.  Using the same card in a PC, the Xserver
  (XFree86-3DLabs-3.3.5-17.i386) reports a maximum allowed dot-clock of
  230MHz, which is correct.

Solution (for RedHat):

  - get the source (XFree86-3.3.5-17.src.rpm) and install it
  - unpack X335src-1.tar.bz2 (in /usr/src/redhat/SOURCES)
  - in xc/programs/Xserver/hw/xfree86/accel/glint/glint.c, change line 1061

      glintInfoRec.dacSpeeds[3] = 110000;

    to

      glintInfoRec.dacSpeeds[3] = 230000;

  - recreate X335src-1.tar.bz2
  - build the binaries (this takes some time):
      rpm -ba /usr/src/redhat/SPECS/XFree86-3.3.5.spec
  - install new server (found in /usr/src/redhat/RPMS/alpha):
      rpm -U --force XFree86-3DLabs-3.3.5-17.alpha.rpm

  The new XF86_3DLabs now reports

  (--) GLINT: Maximum allowed dot-clock: 230.000 MHz

  and one can make full use of the capabilities of the card.

Disclaimer:

  This worked for my particular setup.  Don't try this with any other
  system unless you know what you are doing.





