My first experience with any DEC Alpha was with a (relatively elderly)
AlphaServer 1000 4/233 as Web server. The box has only 64MB of RAM and
is still doing it's job 7/24. What impressed me wasn't the speed (there
wasn't much) but the "torque."  The machine is a tank -- while not fast,
it didn't bog down regardless of the number of things running -- even
disk-intensive ones.

I decided that I wanted one and recently got my own 500MHz 21164a (1MB
L3 cache) and 256MB of RAM for < US$800 (EB164) it's an OEM machine
rather than Digital brand.

MHz ain't everything; the Alpha is capable of executing 4 instructions
per cycle if code has been optimized for the Alpha. The Alpha's floating
point performance is outstanding, so it's ideal for graphics.

Yes, it's possible to get a reasonably good machine for under $1K but
not being Intel there is not the choice of software you are used to. If
you have a job for it, then an Alpha would likely be a good investment
but -- since you can only run *NIX variants or MS-Windows NT you won't
learn much that you can't learn on Intel. 

Happy Holidays -

craig

