Wow. I think you need to look at the really high end Alpha systems. The
largest AlphaServer that I believe you can get (based on memory) is a 
GS120 with something like 2 CPUs with 28Gb of memory. You can increase
the number of CPUs, but as the slots can be used for both, its a 
tradeoff between CPUs and memory. For four CPUs, I think you are probably
going to have a maximum of about 24Gb of memory. You can also hang huge 
amounts of storage of these boxes as well using many different forms of 
SCSI.

You should look at the Compaq website (not good I know), or find someone
in Compaq or a Compaq reseller/VAR who will do this configuration as a
pre-sales task.

You do realise this is going to cost a rather large amount of money?  :-)

> What is the Alpha SMP support like ?

On Tru64, excellent. On Linux, no real information -- I use Linux on a 
PWS433au and thats a uniprocessor box. No experience with other systems,
and I don't even know if the TurboLaser's are supported by Linux.

In all honesty, you are probably going to have to wait for the WildFire
technology to come out to get really large amounts of memory in one 
Alpha system. Have you considered the new Alpha based supercomputers?

Hope this helps!

