R.Gunther <galaxy@rigun.multiweb.nl> wrote in message
news:BRpHOAUaOSpJfZarsUVvTVexRK3x@4ax.com...
> if i come to the point that i type make zImage or bzImage
> i'm getting a error  *** no rule to make 'zImage '. Stop ***
> what is the change between compiling a kernel
> on a 486 (have done it many time on slackware). and a alpha ?
>
> is there some trick ?
>
> greeting richard

On Intel, make zImage and make bzImage compress the kernel
image and attach a decompressor to it (that's that piggyback
thing compiled with (b)zImage). Next, you probably use LILO to
install the new kernel. On Alpha, you simply gzip or bzip2 the
kernel image and copy it to its destination. The decompressor
is in the bootstrap here. The difference is that the PC bootstrap
is very primitive, and only capable of loading a few blocks from
disk (LILO installation among others scans the kernel image
and creates a list of the disk blocks it occupies, so that the
bootstrap can find them). The decompressor must therefore
be in the kernel image, so that the bootstrap can execute it.
On Alpha, the bootstrap has a decompressor built in, and also
it has some knowledge about a filesystem. That means that the
kernel image can be found no matter where it resides on the
disk. The bootstrap is able to search the specified file, load it
and decompress it.
So the installation of a new kernel on Alpha is actually much
simpler. Just compress it (or don't, if you have the space :-) ),
and copy it to the required destination. No LILO installation
is necessary. You only have to pass the kernel image
location to the bootstrap loader (which, no doubt, you already
did, since you were able to compile a kernel)

Martin Sinot
martin@spase.nl





