On Thu, 18 Nov 1999 05:30:01 -0000,
Ian Wilkinson <iwi@sgtwilko.RemoveToMail.freeserve.co.uk> wrote:

> First,
> What does port J12 on the mainboard and J1 on PCI riser with SCSI.
> 
> I was told that the  J1 was a 50 Pin IDE port, but that looks unlikely
> on a SCSI card. I've looked in the Manual and I can't find any
> reference to what sort of interface it is anywhere.  I think that J12
> is IDE, and I think J1 is SCSI-2, but with what I know about SCSI, it
> could be a waffle toaster....

I'm not sure I'm answering your question on this one, but I *have*
fooled around with a 50-pin connector on the SCSI controller on a
Multia.  In fact, it's related to the second question.

I'm a bit unclear here, because I'm not shutting down and peeking inside
to see exactly how things fit together.

I had parts (PCI SCSI controllers) from two Multias -- One had a 50-pin
connector which went to the built-in 2.5" SCSI drive and a huge, blue
plastic "thing" on the side which could connect to an internal drive.
The other one had a much smaller component with the same connection
in the same place, and holes for the same 50-pin connector, but with no
pins in it.  This one came from a Multia which had a 3.5" drive built
into it, and I scrounged the mount rack and the disk from that one also.
The "big" connector wouldn't allow a 3.5" drive in the cabinet, while
the small one would, but then I didn't have a connection for the 2.5"
drive.

I have heard that the SCSI controller in the Multia is incapable of
driving two disks, but I decided to give it a try anyway -- without the
extra disk, the system was more or less useless anyway -- and I had one
of the lab workers solder a 50-pin connector on the scrounged SCSI
controller, mounted the 3.5" drive in the rack (sounds easy, doesn't
it?) connected both disks to the SCSI controller and powered up the
system.  Nothing.  I decided I had broken the controller and let it be.

Later I realised I might have plugged the 50-pin cable in the wrong way.
(boo hiss)  I reversed it, and voil�!  Both drives detected and used.

I don't have the MB or SCSI controller in front of me, but if this is
the 50-pin port J1 you're talking about, then you can indeed connect a
SCSI drive to it.  In my case, I used it for the internal 2.5" drive.

> Second,
> How do you fit a 3.5" SCSI drive in a Multia??

*very* tightly.  In fact, the cable from the 3.5" drive cramps the space
sufficiently that I haven't dared closing the cabinet entirely in fear
of breaking something.  The corner of the cabinet closest to the drive
has a 1-cm gap in it.

> By the looks of things you need a special rack to fit where a PCI card
> would go.  But I don't have one of those.  So maybe the question is
> where do I get one.

I can confirm that you need a rack.  It's shaped more or less like the
cover over an unused PCI-port, but with an angled part with screw holes
for the chassis and the drive.  IIRC, it only supports the drive on one
side.  (Correct me if I'm wrong).  I don't know where you'd find one.

Hope that helps.

> Ian.

